// Practice quiz: depends_on with health checks
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: containers-cicd/containers-and-compose/depends-on-with-health-checks

::o1 what depends_on waits for::With depends_on\: [db] and no condition, what does Compose wait for before starting the dependent service? {
~Until PostgreSQL accepts connections #Not quite. That needs a healthcheck and condition\: service_healthy.
~Until db has run for 30 seconds #Not quite. There is no fixed wait.
=Until the db container is running #Correct. It orders the start but does not wait for readiness.
~Nothing; services start in random order #Not quite. depends_on does order the start.
}

::o1 the race::An API connects to its database at startup and sometimes exits with "connection refused" right after docker compose up. The file has only depends_on\: [db]. Why? {
~depends_on is ignored by docker compose up #Not quite. It is used; it just does not wait for readiness.
~The database port is not published #Not quite. Services reach each other on the container port without publishing.
~The API image is too old #Not quite. The timing of the database start is the cause.
=The API started while PostgreSQL was still initialising #Correct. Running is not the same as ready.
}

::o2 healthcheck for postgres::Which test fits a healthcheck for the postgres\:18 service? {
~["CMD", "curl", "-f", "http\://localhost\:5432"] #Not quite. PostgreSQL does not speak HTTP.
~["CMD", "ls", "/var/lib/postgresql"] #Not quite. A folder existing says nothing about accepting connections.
=["CMD-SHELL", "pg_isready -U $$\{POSTGRES_USER\} -d $$\{POSTGRES_DB\}"] #Correct. pg_isready exits 0 when the server accepts connections.
~["CMD-SHELL", "exit 0"] #Not quite. It always passes, so it checks nothing.
}

::o2 wait condition::Which depends_on entry makes the api service wait for a healthy db? {
~db\: condition\: service_started #Not quite. That waits only until db is running.
~db\: condition\: service_completed_successfully #Not quite. That waits for a one-off task to finish.
~db\: restart\: true #Not quite. That restarts api when db restarts; it sets no wait.
=db\: condition\: service_healthy #Correct. Compose starts api only after db reports healthy.
}

::o3 read starting::docker compose ps shows db as "Up 5 seconds (health\: starting)". What does it mean? {
~The database is ready #Not quite. Ready shows as (healthy).
=The container runs, but no health check has passed yet #Correct. The state changes to healthy after a passing check.
~The health check failed repeatedly #Not quite. That shows as (unhealthy).
~The container is restarting #Not quite. It is up; only its health is still being decided.
}

::o3 read unhealthy::docker compose ps shows "Up 3 minutes (unhealthy)". Which statement is true? {
=The health check failed the configured number of times in a row; the container still runs #Correct. Unhealthy is a health state, not a stopped container.
~The container has stopped #Not quite. It is still Up.
~The check has not run yet #Not quite. That shows as (health\: starting).
~Compose will start services that wait for service_healthy anyway #Not quite. They do not start while db is unhealthy.
}
